---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import FutureValueCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc/FutureValueCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="future-value-calculator">
  <FutureValueCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the future value calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The future value calculator grows a starting amount and a series of equal payments at a fixed rate.
      Each period, interest is added to the balance, and the next period's interest is on the larger
      balance.
    </p>
    <p>
      The formula is <code>FV = PV × (1 + i)^n + PMT × ((1 + i)^n − 1) ÷ i</code>. <code>PV</code> is the
      starting amount, <code>PMT</code> is the payment each period, <code>i</code> is the rate per payment
      period and <code>n</code> is the number of payments. When the payments come at the start of each
      period, the payment part is multiplied by <code>(1 + i)</code>. When the compounding and payment
      frequencies differ, the rate per payment period is <code>i = (1 + r ÷ m)^(m ÷ p) − 1</code>, with
      <code>m</code> compounding periods and <code>p</code> payments a year.
    </p>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Start with 10,000 and pay in 200 at the end of each month for 15 years, at 6% a year compounded
      monthly. The rate per month is 0.5% and there are 180 payments. The starting amount grows to 24,540.94
      and the payments grow to 58,163.74, so the future value is 82,704.68. You pay in 46,000; the other
      36,704.68 is interest. With the payments at the start of each month, the future value is 82,995.50.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The rate is the same for the whole time and the payments are all the same size. Years are rounded to
      whole payment periods. Tax, fees and inflation are not included.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
